        b638_q('b633-ree-math-complex-phasors','Evaluate (2+j)(1−j).','3−j',['1−j','3+j','2−3j'],'Expand: 2−2j+j−j²=3−j because j²=−1.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','complex-product','impedance-combination','uses j²=+1','(2+j)*(1-j)=3-j'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-complex-phasors','What is the complex conjugate of −3+j5?','−3−j5',['3−j5','−3+j5','3+j5'],'Conjugation preserves the real part and reverses the imaginary sign.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','complex-conjugation','phasor-algebra','changes the real sign instead',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-complex-phasors','Divide 10∠50° by 2∠20°.','5∠30°',['5∠70°','20∠30°','8∠30°'],'Divide magnitudes and subtract denominator angle: 10/2=5 and 50°−20°=30°.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','polar-complex-division','phasor-ratio','adds angles during division','10/2=5;50-20=30'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-complex-phasors','A current phasor is 8∠0° A and voltage is 120∠−30° V. What is the voltage angle relative to current?','30° lagging',['30° leading','0°','90° lagging'],'Voltage at −30° relative to current at 0° lags by 30°.','D3','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','phasor-relative-angle','phase-interpretation','reads the sign as leading',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-calculus-differential','If v(t)=5t²−2t, what is dv/dt at t=2?','18',['8','20','22'],'Differentiate to get 10t−2; at t=2 the derivative is 18.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','polynomial-derivative','rate-of-change','substitutes before differentiating','10*2-2=18'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-calculus-differential','Integrate a constant 3 A current from t=1 s to t=4 s. What charge is transferred?','9 C',['12 C','3 C','1 C'],'The interval is 3 s, so Q=IΔt=3(4−1)=9 C.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','definite-current-integral','charge-interval','uses the final time rather than elapsed time','3*(4-1)=9C'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-calculus-differential','For f(x)=x²−8x+20, at what x does f reach its minimum?','4',['−4','8','2'],'Set f′=2x−8=0 to obtain x=4; f″=2>0 confirms a minimum.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','quadratic-optimization','loss-minimization','sets the function value, not derivative, to zero','2*x-8=0;x=4'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-calculus-differential','A first-order model is dx/dt=−2x. If x(0)>0, what happens to x over time?','It decays exponentially toward zero',['It grows without bound','It remains constant','It oscillates at a fixed frequency'],'The negative real coefficient gives a stable exponential e^(−2t), not growth or oscillation.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','first-order-decay','dynamic-model-interpretation','ignores the sign of the system coefficient',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-calculus-differential','A rotor angle is θ(t)=4t³ radians. What is angular velocity at t=1 s?','12 rad/s',['4 rad/s','7 rad/s','16 rad/s'],'ω=dθ/dt=12t²; at t=1 it is 12 rad/s.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','angular-velocity-derivative','rotor-motion','reports angular position as rate','12*1^2=12rad/s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-probability-statistics','A load is 2 kW with probability 0.25 and 6 kW otherwise. What is expected load?','5 kW',['4 kW','6 kW','3.5 kW'],'E[X]=0.25(2)+0.75(6)=5 kW.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','discrete-expectation','load-uncertainty','uses the simple midpoint without probabilities','0.25*2+0.75*6=5kW'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-probability-statistics','For independent events with probabilities 0.2 and 0.5, what is the probability both occur?','0.10',['0.70','0.30','0.25'],'Independence gives P(A∩B)=P(A)P(B)=0.2(0.5)=0.10.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','independent-intersection','event-combination','adds independent-event probabilities','0.2*0.5=0.10'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-probability-statistics','A measurement set has mean 40 and standard deviation 5. What is the z-score for 50?','2',['1','5','10'],'z=(50−40)/5=2 standard deviations above the mean.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','standard-score','measurement-interpretation','divides by the mean instead of standard deviation','(50-40)/5=2'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-probability-statistics','A sample mean becomes more precise as sample size increases, assuming independent observations and constant variance. How does its standard error change?','It decreases in proportion to 1/√n',['It increases in proportion to n','It remains equal to the population standard deviation','It becomes zero for any n>1'],'The standard error of the mean is σ/√n under the stated assumptions.','D3','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','standard-error-scaling','sampling-uncertainty','confuses standard deviation of data with standard error of mean',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-probability-statistics','Two independent components have reliabilities 0.95 and 0.90 in series. What is system reliability?','0.855',['0.925','0.05','0.95'],'Both must work, so multiply: 0.95×0.90=0.855.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','series-system-reliability','reliability-model','uses the better component reliability alone','0.95*0.90=0.855'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-discrete-economics','What is the future worth of 1,000 invested for two years at 10% compounded annually?','1,210',['1,200','1,100','1,331'],'F=P(1+i)²=1000(1.1)²=1,210.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','compound-future-worth','capital-growth','applies simple interest for two years','1000*1.1^2=1210'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-discrete-economics','A project costs 5,000 now and returns 2,000 at each year-end for three years. Ignoring discounting, what is the net total?','1,000',['−1,000','6,000','11,000'],'Total returns are 3×2,000=6,000; net is 6,000−5,000=1,000.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','undiscounted-project-net','project-economics','subtracts the return from the cost in reverse order','3*2000-5000=1000'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-discrete-economics','At 8% annual interest, what is the one-year discount factor?','1/1.08',['1.08','0.08','1−0.08'],'A present-worth factor for one year is 1/(1+i), here 1/1.08.','D2','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','discount-factor','present-worth-model','uses the accumulation factor as a discount factor',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-discrete-economics','A device costs 24,000 and has a 6,000 salvage value after six years. What is straight-line annual depreciation?','3,000 per year',['4,000 per year','5,000 per year','18,000 per year'],'Depreciable basis is 24,000−6,000=18,000; divide by six years to get 3,000/year.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','straight-line-depreciation','asset-costing','depreciates the salvage value too','(24000-6000)/6=3000'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-discrete-economics','Two mutually exclusive projects have equal annual service and lives. Which criterion directly compares their discounted total costs?','Present worth at the common interest rate',['Undiscounted first cost only','Number of components','Peak load alone'],'Present worth converts dated costs to a common time basis at the stated rate.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','present-worth-comparison','alternative-selection','ignores timing of cash flows',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-discrete-economics','A cost rises from 10,000 to 10,800. What percentage increase is this?','8%',['0.8%','80%','108%'],'The increase is 800; 800/10,000×100=8%.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','percentage-change','cost-trend','divides by the new value','800/10000*100=8%'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-discrete-economics','A maintenance plan costs 1,200 each year for five years, with no discounting. What total cost should be compared?','6,000',['1,200','5,000','7,200'],'With equal annual costs and no discounting, total is 5×1,200=6,000.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','uniform-series-total-undiscounted','maintenance-economics','multiplies by cost period incorrectly','5*1200=6000'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-discrete-economics','When comparing projects with different service lives, why may a common analysis period or equivalent annual measure be needed?','To avoid comparing unequal durations as if they were equivalent',['To remove all future costs','To make discount rates equal zero','To ignore replacement needs'],'A common basis accounts for service duration and potential replacement cycles.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','unequal-life-comparison','life-cycle-choice','compares unequal lives using one-time costs only',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-trigonometry-analytic-geometry','A 13-unit vector has x-component 5. If its y-component is positive, what is the y-component magnitude?','12',['8','18','√18'],'From x²+y²=13², y=√(169−25)=12.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','vector-component-recovery','orthogonal-components','subtracts magnitudes rather than squared components','sqrt(13^2-5^2)=12'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-trigonometry-analytic-geometry','A point is 4 units from the origin at 30° above the x-axis. What is its y-coordinate?','2',['3.46','4','−2'],'y=r sinθ=4 sin30°=2.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','polar-y-component','coordinate-transform','uses cosine for the vertical component','4*sin(30deg)=2'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-trigonometry-analytic-geometry','For vectors a=(2,−1) and b=(1,3), what is a·b?','−1',['5','1','−5'],'The dot product is 2(1)+(−1)(3)=−1. Its sign indicates an obtuse angle between the vectors.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','vector-dot-product','vector-operation','multiplies magnitudes or omits the negative component','2*1+(-1)*3=-1'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-trigonometry-analytic-geometry','A line has slope −2. What does the sign indicate as x increases?','y decreases',['y increases','y remains constant','The line is vertical'],'A negative slope means y changes downward as x increases.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','slope-direction','line-interpretation','uses slope magnitude but ignores its sign',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-trigonometry-analytic-geometry','What is the distance between points (1,2) and (4,6)?','5',['7','4','25'],'Distance is √[(4−1)²+(6−2)²]=√25=5.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','coordinate-distance','spatial-measure','adds coordinate differences without squaring','sqrt((4-1)^2+(6-2)^2)=5'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-trigonometry-analytic-geometry','For a right triangle, cosθ is defined as which ratio?','Adjacent side over hypotenuse',['Opposite side over hypotenuse','Opposite over adjacent','Hypotenuse over adjacent'],'Cosine relates the side adjacent to θ to the hypotenuse.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','cosine-ratio','triangle-model','swaps sine and cosine ratios',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-trigonometry-analytic-geometry','A point at (3,4) is rotated 90° counterclockwise about the origin. What are its new coordinates?','(−4,3)',['(4,−3)','(3,−4)','(−3,4)'],'A 90° counterclockwise rotation maps (x,y) to (−y,x).','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','coordinate-rotation','geometry-transform','uses clockwise rotation mapping','(3,4)->(-4,3)'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-matrices-determinants','Solve x+y=7 and x−y=1. What is x?','4',['3','6','8'],'Adding the equations gives 2x=8, so x=4.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','two-equation-elimination','linear-system','subtracts equations with inconsistent signs','(7+1)/2=4'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-matrices-determinants','If A is invertible and Ax=b, how is x obtained?','x=A⁻¹b',['x=Ab','x=bA⁻¹ in every convention','x=A+b'],'Premultiplying Ax=b by A⁻¹ gives x=A⁻¹b.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','inverse-system-solution','linear-algebra-method','multiplies by inverse on the wrong side',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-matrices-determinants','What is det([[2,0],[0,5]])?','10',['7','5','0'],'For a diagonal matrix the determinant is the product of diagonal entries: 2×5=10.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','diagonal-determinant','matrix-invariant','adds diagonal entries','2*5=10'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-matrices-determinants','A system of three independent linear equations has three unknowns. What does independence imply for its coefficient matrix?','It has full rank and a unique solution for any consistent right-hand side',['It must be singular','It has rank one','It has no solution'],'Independent rows give full rank; for a square system the matrix is invertible and yields a unique solution.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','rank-and-uniqueness','system-structure','confuses more equations with dependence',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-matrices-determinants','For A=[[1,2],[0,1]], what is det(A)?','1',['0','2','−1'],'The determinant is 1(1)−2(0)=1.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','triangular-determinant','matrix-evaluation','multiplies all entries','1*1-2*0=1'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-matrices-determinants','When the same elementary row operation is applied to both sides of an augmented system, what is preserved?','The set of solutions',['The numerical values of every matrix entry','The determinant under every operation','The number of written rows'],'Elementary row operations produce an equivalent system with the same solution set.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','equivalent-augmented-system','elimination','assumes entries must remain unchanged',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-matrices-determinants','A is 2×3 and B is 3×1. What is the size of AB?','2×1',['3×3','1×2','2×3'],'The inner dimensions match and the product has the outer dimensions: 2×1.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','matrix-product-dimensions','matrix-compatibility','uses inner dimensions as product dimensions',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-sequences-series','An arithmetic sequence has a₁=4 and d=−2. What is a₆?','−6',['−8','14','6'],'a₆=4+5(−2)=−6.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','arithmetic-decreasing-term','sequence-model','uses six differences instead of five','4+5*(-2)=-6'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-sequences-series','A geometric sequence has a₁=81 and r=1/3. What is a₄?','3',['9','1','27'],'a₄=81(1/3)³=3.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','geometric-decay-term','decaying-sequence','uses exponent n rather than n−1','81*(1/3)^3=3'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-sequences-series','An arithmetic series has 8 terms, first term 2 and last term 16. What is its sum?','72',['64','18','144'],'S=n(a₁+aₙ)/2=8(18)/2=72.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','arithmetic-series-sum','accumulated-output','uses term difference as term count','8*(2+16)/2=72'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-sequences-series','A geometric series has first term 6 and ratio 0.5. What is its infinite sum?','12',['3','6','∞'],'Since |r|<1, S∞=a₁/(1−r)=6/0.5=12.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','infinite-geometric-sum','decaying-response','uses 1+r in the denominator','6/(1-.5)=12'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-sequences-series','A recurrence is xₙ₊₁=xₙ+4 with x₀=−1. What is x₃?','11',['7','3','15'],'Apply the update three times: −1+3(4)=11.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','additive-recurrence','iterative-update','counts only two updates','-1+3*4=11'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-sequences-series','A sequence approaches zero with alternating signs. Which condition supports convergence of its geometric model?','The common ratio magnitude is below one',['The common ratio is greater than one','The common ratio is negative only','The first term is negative'],'A geometric sequence tends to zero when |r|<1; a negative ratio only alternates sign.','D2','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','geometric-limit-condition','alternating-response','mistakes alternating sign for convergence criterion',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-sequences-series','The partial sums of a series approach a finite limit. What does that establish?','The series converges',['Every term is positive','The terms are constant','The series has finitely many terms'],'Convergence is defined by a finite limit of the sequence of partial sums.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','partial-sum-convergence','series-limit','confuses term behavior with partial-sum definition',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-fourier-laplace','What is the Laplace transform of a unit step u(t)?','1/s',['s','1','e^(−s)'],'The unit step is 1 for t≥0, so its transform is ∫₀∞e⁻ˢᵗdt=1/s.','D2','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','unit-step-transform','switching-input','treats the step as an impulse','integral=1/s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-fourier-laplace','A first-order transfer function is H(s)=5/(s+5). What is its pole?','−5',['5','0','−1/5'],'The pole is the root of the denominator s+5=0, so s=−5.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','first-order-pole','system-identification','uses the time constant as the pole','s+5=0 => -5'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-fourier-laplace','For an LTI system, what operation combines an input with the impulse response to produce the output?','Convolution',['Correlation only','Differentiation in all cases','Division by the impulse response'],'The time-domain input-output relation of an LTI system is convolution.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','lti-input-output','system-modeling','confuses convolution with correlation',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-fourier-laplace','A sinusoid has frequency 60 Hz. What is its angular frequency approximately?','377 rad/s',['60 rad/s','120 rad/s','188 rad/s'],'ω=2πf=2π(60)≈377 rad/s.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','frequency-angular-frequency','sinusoidal-model','omits the factor 2π','2*pi*60=377rad/s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-fourier-laplace','A stable causal rational system has all poles in which region of the s-plane?','Left half-plane',['Right half-plane','On the positive real axis only','At infinity'],'For a continuous-time stable system, poles must have negative real parts.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','pole-stability-location','stability-analysis','uses discrete-time unit-circle condition for continuous-time s-plane',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-fourier-laplace','A periodic waveform has a strong third harmonic and a 50 Hz fundamental. What is the third-harmonic frequency?','150 Hz',['53 Hz','100 Hz','200 Hz'],'The nth harmonic is n times the fundamental: 3×50=150 Hz.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','harmonic-frequency','spectrum-interpretation','adds the harmonic number to frequency','3*50=150Hz'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-math-fourier-laplace','An odd periodic function is represented by a Fourier series. Which coefficient family vanishes by symmetry?','The cosine coefficients and constant term',['The sine coefficients only','All coefficients','The fundamental sine coefficient'],'Odd symmetry cancels the constant and cosine integrals over a symmetric period.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','fourier-odd-symmetry','waveform-symmetry','reverses odd/even coefficient rules',''),
