        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-fluid-mechanics','In steady incompressible flow, if pipe area halves, what happens to average velocity when flow is conserved?','It doubles',['It halves','It is unchanged','It becomes four times greater'],'Continuity Av=constant; halving A doubles v.','D3','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','continuity-area-change','constricted-flow','assumes equal velocity at unequal areas',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-fluid-mechanics','A 0.04 m³ volume of water is displaced. What buoyant force acts using ρ=1000 kg/m³ and g=10 m/s²?','400 N',['40 N','4,000 N','0.4 N'],'F_b=ρgV=1000(10)(0.04)=400 N.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','buoyant-force','displaced-volume','uses object mass instead of displaced-fluid volume','1000*10*.04=400N'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-fluid-mechanics','A pump raises water to a higher elevation. Which ideal energy term increases?','Potential head',['Velocity head must vanish','Pressure head is always zero','The fluid density doubles'],'The elevation component of mechanical energy increases; pressure and velocity terms depend on the rest of the system.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','hydraulic-energy-head','pump-system','assumes elevation change alone fixes every other head term',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-fluid-mechanics','Why does a real pipe require more pump head than an ideal lossless pipe for the same delivery?','Friction and local losses dissipate mechanical energy',['The fluid gains energy from friction','The pipe wall creates pressure for free','Continuity ceases to apply'],'Real flow has frictional and fitting losses that the pump must overcome.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','head-loss-accounting','pump-selection','treats friction as an energy source',''),
