    $add([
        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-instrumentation','A sensor produces 2 V at 10 units and 6 V at 30 units. Assuming linear response, what is sensitivity?','0.2 V/unit',['0.1 V/unit','2 V/unit','4 V/unit'],'Sensitivity is Δoutput/Δinput=(6−2)/(30−10)=0.2 V/unit.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','sensor-sensitivity','calibration-curve','uses output value rather than change','(6-2)/(30-10)=.2'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-instrumentation','A voltmeter has finite input resistance connected across a high-resistance source. What can happen?','The meter loads the source and changes the measured voltage',['The source resistance becomes zero','The input signal is amplified without power','The meter removes all noise'],'Finite input resistance forms a divider with source resistance, perturbing the measured node.','D3','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','meter-loading','instrument-connection','assumes measurement is noninvasive',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-instrumentation','A meter is specified as ±1% of reading. At a 200 V reading, what is the magnitude of the stated uncertainty?','±2 V',['±1 V','±20 V','±0.02 V'],'One percent of 200 V is 2 V.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','percent-reading-uncertainty','instrument-accuracy','applies percent to full scale without that specification','0.01*200=2V'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-instrumentation','A measurement has high repeatability but a consistent offset from a reference. Which term describes the main issue?','Bias',['Random noise only','Resolution','Sampling rate'],'A consistent systematic offset indicates bias, even if repeated readings cluster tightly.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','systematic-bias','measurement-quality','equates repeatability with accuracy',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-allied-instrumentation','A two-channel oscilloscope subtracts channel 2 from channel 1. What must be considered for valid common-mode rejection?','Channel gain and timing matching',['Only display brightness','The color of probe leads','The number of trigger buttons'],'Mismatch in gain or delay leaves residual common-mode signal after subtraction.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','differential-measurement-matching','scope-interpretation','assumes subtraction cancels common mode perfectly despite channel mismatch',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-generators-conversion','A generator produces 18 kW electrical output at 90% efficiency. What mechanical input is required?','20 kW',['16.2 kW','19.8 kW','10 kW'],'η=Pout/Pin, so Pin=18/0.90=20 kW.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','generator-input-from-efficiency','machine-energy-balance','multiplies output by efficiency','18/.9=20kW'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-generators-conversion','A synchronous generator’s rotor field is excited by what in the basic model?','A DC field current or equivalent excitation source',['The stator load current alone','Mechanical friction','The prime mover exhaust'],'The rotor magnetic field is established by excitation; the prime mover supplies mechanical input.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','synchronous-generator-excitation','machine-construction','confuses field excitation with shaft input',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-generators-conversion','An alternator is driven faster while pole count is fixed. What happens to generated electrical frequency?','It increases in proportion to speed',['It decreases to zero','It remains fixed','It depends only on load resistance'],'For a synchronous machine, frequency is proportional to mechanical speed times pole pairs.','D3','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','speed-frequency-proportionality','generator-operation','treats frequency as independent of rotor speed',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-generators-conversion','A prime mover torque is 50 N·m at 100 rad/s. What mechanical power is delivered?','5 kW',['500 W','50 kW','0.5 kW'],'P=Tω=50(100)=5,000 W=5 kW.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','shaft-power-torque-speed','machine-input','uses rpm/rad-s conversion despite rad/s already given','50*100=5000W'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-generators-conversion','Why does increasing generator load generally require greater prime-mover torque to maintain speed?','The electrical output power and internal losses require added mechanical input',['The load creates mechanical energy','The rotor field disappears at higher load','The shaft torque is unrelated to power'],'Maintaining speed under greater converted power requires increased mechanical power and torque.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','load-torque-balance','generator-loading','assumes electrical load has no mechanical input consequence',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-analysis','A 3-phase system has 12 kV line voltage and 60 MVA base. What is line-quantity base impedance?','2.4 Ω',['0.2 Ω','24 Ω','4.8 Ω'],'Zbase=VLL²/S3φ=(12,000)²/60,000,000=2.4 Ω.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','three-phase-base-impedance','per-unit-base','mixes phase voltage and three-phase base inconsistently','12000^2/60000000=2.4'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-analysis','A system has 1.0 pu voltage across 0.25 pu reactance. What is idealized fault-current magnitude?','4 pu',['0.25 pu','1.25 pu','2 pu'],'Using I=V/X, 1.0/0.25=4 pu under the stated simplified Thevenin model.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','per-unit-fault-current','fault-screening','multiplies voltage and reactance','1/.25=4pu'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-analysis','A base changes from 100 MVA to 50 MVA while voltage base stays fixed. How does the per-unit value of a fixed physical impedance change?','Half the original per-unit value',['Twice the original value','Unchanged','Four times the original'],'With voltage base fixed, halving base MVA doubles Zbase; a fixed physical impedance therefore has half its pu value.','D4','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','per-unit-base-change','base-conversion','reverses base impedance and per-unit scaling','Zpu_new/Zpu_old=50/100=0.5'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-analysis','A one-line diagram is used to identify which bus a breaker isolates. What should the analyst confirm before a fault calculation?','The network topology and equipment data match the modeled case',['That every breaker is closed','That load power factor is exactly one','That the diagram is drawn to scale'],'A valid fault model depends on correct topology, impedance, and source data; a drawing is not automatically a complete model.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','fault-model-input-check','network-analysis','assumes a visual one-line alone provides all numerical parameters',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-wiring-utilization','A 120 V load draws 5 A. What is its apparent power if single-phase?','600 VA',['24 VA','125 VA','600 W regardless of power factor'],'Single-phase apparent power S=VI=120(5)=600 VA; real power also needs power factor.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','single-phase-apparent-power','load-schedule','labels apparent power as real power without pf','120*5=600VA'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-wiring-utilization','A load uses 2 kW for 3 h. How much electrical energy is consumed?','6 kWh',['0.67 kWh','5 kWh','6 kW'],'Energy is power times time: 2×3=6 kWh.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','load-energy-use','energy-accounting','reports power rather than energy','2*3=6kWh'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-wiring-utilization','A luminaire flux is 1,200 lm distributed uniformly over 20 m² in an idealized estimate. What average illuminance results?','60 lx',['24 lx','1,200 lx','0.017 lx'],'Average illuminance is flux per area: 1200/20=60 lm/m²=60 lx.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','luminous-flux-area','lighting-estimate','multiplies flux by area','1200/20=60lx'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-wiring-utilization','A conductor route is longer than the drawing assumed. With the same material and area, what happens to its resistance?','It increases in proportion to length',['It decreases','It is unchanged','It becomes inversely proportional to resistivity'],'R=ρL/A, so resistance increases with length when other terms are fixed.','D3','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','length-resistance-trend','route-review','ignores the length term',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-wiring-utilization','A design calculation estimates 5% voltage drop, but no applicable project limit or governing edition is provided. What can be concluded?','The calculated drop can be reported, but compliance cannot be asserted',['It automatically complies','It automatically violates every code','The result should be hidden'],'A numerical result is not a compliance conclusion without the controlling requirement and applicable conditions.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','calculation-versus-compliance','design-review','confuses an engineering estimate with a verified code limit',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-protection-relaying','A relay sees current above its pickup setting. What does pickup indicate?','The measured quantity crossed the relay’s operating threshold',['The breaker has already opened','The fault is definitely outside every protected zone','The relay has cleared the fault energy'],'Pickup initiates or enables the relay operating logic; it is not itself proof that interruption occurred.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','relay-pickup-meaning','relay-operation','confuses detection with breaker interruption',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-protection-relaying','Why is backup protection provided?','To clear a fault if the primary protection or breaker fails',['To make the primary relay unnecessary','To ensure every relay trips first','To increase normal load current'],'Backup provides another clearing path after a primary-device failure, typically with coordination delay.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','protection-backup-purpose','reliability-design','assumes primary protection never fails',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-protection-relaying','A CT ratio is 200:5. What secondary current corresponds to 80 A primary current?','2 A',['16 A','4 A','0.5 A'],'The ratio is 40:1, so 80/40=2 A.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','current-transformer-scaling','relay-measurement','uses the ratio in the opposite direction','80/(200/5)=2A'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-protection-relaying','Two relays have the same pickup but different intentional delays. Which is generally the backup in a coordinated pair?','The slower relay farther from the protected primary zone',['The faster relay in every case','The relay with no trip contact','The relay with the lower voltage rating'],'Coordination typically allows the primary closest to the fault to operate first and the upstream backup later.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','time-coordination-role','selective-protection','selects backup solely by pickup setting',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-protection-relaying','A differential relay compares currents entering and leaving a protected zone. What condition suggests an internal fault in the ideal model?','A nonzero differential current beyond the restraint logic',['Equal entering and leaving currents','Zero current everywhere','Only a voltage rise outside the zone'],'An internal fault creates a mismatch in the zone current balance; practical relays also use restraint and CT checks.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','differential-zone-balance','protection-principle','assumes through-current indicates internal fault',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-transients','An RC circuit has R=10 kΩ and C=20 μF. What is its time constant?','0.20 s',['2 s','0.02 s','200 s'],'τ=RC=10,000×20×10⁻⁶=0.20 s.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','rc-time-constant','first-order-transient','misses microfarad conversion','10000*20e-6=.2s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-transients','An RL circuit has L=2 H and R=4 Ω. What is its time constant?','0.5 s',['8 s','2 s','0.25 s'],'τ=L/R=2/4=0.5 s.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','rl-time-constant','first-order-transient','uses RC relation instead of L/R','2/4=.5s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-transients','At the instant a DC voltage is applied to an ideal uncharged capacitor, what is its initial voltage?','Zero',['Equal to the source immediately in every circuit','Infinite','Undefined because capacitance blocks all change'],'Capacitor voltage cannot change instantaneously; an initially uncharged capacitor begins at zero volts.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','capacitor-initial-condition','switching-response','uses long-time open-circuit behavior at t=0+',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-transients','An inductor current is 3 A just before switching. What is the current immediately after switching in an ideal circuit?','3 A',['0 A','It doubles','It changes sign automatically'],'Inductor current is continuous across an ideal switching instant unless an impulse voltage is present.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','inductor-current-continuity','switching-response','treats inductor current as able to jump instantaneously',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-transients','A first-order response reaches 95% of its final value at about 3τ. If τ=0.2 s, what time is this?','0.6 s',['0.067 s','3.2 s','1.5 s'],'3τ=3(0.2)=0.6 s, approximately 95% for a first-order step.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','first-order-settling-estimate','response-timing','uses τ/3 instead of 3τ','3*.2=.6s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-control-systems','A feedback controller applies too much correction and causes sustained oscillation. Which change may reduce aggressiveness?','Reduce loop gain or retune the controller using stability analysis',['Increase gain without checking margins','Remove all feedback sensors','Increase the reference step'],'Excessive loop gain can reduce stability margin; retuning should be guided by a model and response evidence.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','loop-gain-stability-tradeoff','controller-tuning','assumes higher gain always improves response',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-control-systems','A stable continuous-time pole is at s=−4. What is the associated first-order time constant?','0.25 s',['4 s','−0.25 s','2 s'],'For a pole at −1/τ, τ=1/4=0.25 s.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','pole-time-constant','system-identification','uses pole magnitude directly as time constant','1/4=.25s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-control-systems','A controller output saturates at its limit while integral action continues accumulating error. What problem can follow?','Integrator windup',['Aliasing','Thermal runaway in the sensor','Perfect zero steady-state error'],'When actuator output saturates, continued integral accumulation can cause windup and slow recovery.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','integrator-windup','actuator-saturation','assumes controller command is always physically achievable',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-control-systems','A system output changes in the same direction as its error correction instead of opposing the disturbance. What sign issue should be checked?','Feedback polarity at the summing junction',['Sampling interval only','Sensor resolution only','Plant color coding'],'Incorrect feedback sign can turn negative feedback into positive feedback and destabilize the loop.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','feedback-polarity','loop-diagnosis','checks gain magnitude but not sign',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-control-systems','A closed-loop transfer function has a characteristic equation 1+K=0 for positive K. Does it have a positive real K solution?','No',['Yes, K=1','Yes, K=0.5','Every positive K is a solution'],'1+K=0 requires K=−1, so no positive real K satisfies it.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','characteristic-equation-root','stability-equation','drops the constant term','1+K=0 => K=-1'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-electronics','An ideal buck converter has Vin=48 V and duty ratio 0.25. What is its average output voltage?','12 V',['24 V','36 V','192 V'],'For an ideal buck, Vout=DVin=0.25(48)=12 V.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','buck-conversion-ratio','dc-step-down','uses boost ratio for a buck stage','.25*48=12V'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-electronics','An ideal boost converter has Vin=12 V and D=0.5. What output voltage is predicted?','24 V',['6 V','18 V','12 V'],'Vout=Vin/(1−D)=12/0.5=24 V.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','boost-conversion-ratio','dc-step-up','uses buck relation instead of boost relation','12/(1-.5)=24V'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-electronics','A diode in a rectifier blocks reverse current in the ideal model. What function does it provide?','Unidirectional conduction',['Voltage amplification','Frequency multiplication by itself','Energy storage'],'The diode conducts in one direction and blocks the other, enabling rectification.','D2','NON_NUMERICAL','rectifier-diode-function','power-conversion','assumes diode alone smooths the output',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-electronics','A converter’s output ripple is too large after reducing its filter capacitance. What direction of change generally reduces ripple, all else equal?','Increase effective capacitance',['Decrease capacitance further','Increase load current without limit','Remove the filter'],'More stored charge per voltage change generally reduces ripple, subject to topology and operating constraints.','D3','CONCEPTUAL_QUANTITATIVE','capacitor-ripple-trend','filter-design','assumes less storage smooths more',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-power-electronics','Why does a real converter’s output differ from an ideal duty-ratio prediction?','Device drops, losses, ripple, and operating mode affect the result',['The ideal equation includes all parasitics','The output is independent of load','Real switches have no resistance'],'Ideal conversion ratios omit nonideal device and operating effects; predictions require assumptions.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','ideal-nonideal-converter-gap','model-limits','treats ideal model as exact hardware behavior',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-telecommunications','A binary channel transmits 2400 bits in 2 s. What is the bit rate?','1200 bit/s',['4800 bit/s','2400 bit/s','1200 baud always'],'Bit rate is 2400/2=1200 bit/s; baud equality depends on bits per symbol.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','bit-rate-from-count','digital-communication','equates bit/s and baud without modulation detail','2400/2=1200bit/s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-telecommunications','A signal’s amplitude is unchanged but noise power doubles. What happens to SNR in dB?','It decreases by about 3 dB',['It increases by 3 dB','It is unchanged','It decreases by 20 dB'],'Doubling noise halves the linear SNR, a 10log₁₀(1/2)≈−3 dB change.','D4','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','snr-noise-doubling','link-quality','uses 20log for a power ratio','10*log10(.5)≈-3dB'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-telecommunications','A low-pass channel suppresses frequencies above its passband. What happens to a sharp pulse containing high-frequency components?','Its edges become slower or more rounded',['Its peak frequency becomes infinite','It becomes a perfect impulse','Its duration must become zero'],'Removing high-frequency content smooths rapid transitions and broadens edges in time.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','bandwidth-time-shape','channel-filtering','assumes filtering changes only amplitude scale',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-telecommunications','A forward-error-correcting code adds redundant bits. What tradeoff does it introduce?','Higher robustness with lower useful-data fraction at fixed raw channel rate',['No change in throughput or reliability','More noise power in the channel','Elimination of all possible errors'],'Redundancy can enable error correction but consumes channel capacity or lowers net information rate.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','coding-redundancy-tradeoff','communication-reliability','assumes coding gives free reliability',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-telecommunications','A channel has bandwidth 4 kHz and an ideal baseband signal uses the full band. What sampling boundary follows under the ideal Nyquist assumption?','8 ksample/s',['4 ksample/s','2 ksample/s','16 ksample/s'],'The ideal sampling boundary is twice the highest frequency: 2×4 kHz=8 ksample/s.','D3','CALCULATION_REQUIRED','sampling-boundary','digitization','uses bandwidth itself as the sampling rate','2*4=8ksample/s'),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-computer-power-applications','A load-flow result changes dramatically after one bus voltage is entered in kV instead of V. What should the software do?','Validate units and plausible ranges before solving',['Silently accept any numeric magnitude','Round all inputs to the nearest integer','Use the last cached result without warning'],'Unit-aware range validation can catch scale errors before they corrupt system calculations.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','unit-range-validation','power-model-input','assumes a number has a universal unit',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-computer-power-applications','An operator reviews a contingency result. Which paired data should be checked before acting on it?','Case version and topology/status assumptions',['Only screen resolution','The order of menu buttons','A prior result from another network'],'The result is conditional on the modeled case, topology, and equipment status; verify those assumptions.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','case-provenance-check','contingency-operations','treats results as independent of modeled state',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-computer-power-applications','A monitoring system reports a negative load where the sign convention defines consumption positive. What is the first diagnostic?','Check measurement polarity and sign convention',['Delete all negative values','Assume generation without checking','Change the sign in the report only'],'A sign-convention or transducer-polarity error can reverse interpretation; verify measurement setup and definitions.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','power-sign-convention','telemetry-diagnosis','corrects display without checking the measurement chain',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-computer-power-applications','A load forecast is evaluated against held-out historical periods. What is the main reason for holding out data?','To assess performance on data not used to fit the model',['To make the training error exactly zero','To remove the need for input validation','To increase the number of measured samples'],'Held-out evaluation tests generalization rather than fit to training data.','D3','NON_NUMERICAL','out-of-sample-validation','forecast-evaluation','confuses training fit with predictive performance',''),
        b638_q('b633-ree-pro-computer-power-applications','A control-room application loses network connection but retains local protection. What design principle is demonstrated?','Safety-critical local functions should fail safely and not depend solely on remote communications',['All protection must stop when communications fail','Remote control is always safer','Network loss proves a physical fault'],'Local protective functions and safe fallback behavior reduce dependence on a communication path.','D4','NON_NUMERICAL','communication-loss-fallback','operational-resilience','assumes communication availability is guaranteed',''),
    ]);
    return $q;
}

